{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 42, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades a powerful and influential ruler known for his military prowess political acumen and complex relationships with both Christian and Muslim powers. Raymond inherited the County from his father Raymond II at a young age facing immediate challenges to his rule from internal dissent and external threats. His reign was marked by constant warfare and diplomatic maneuvering aimed at securing Tripoli’s independence and prosperity.\n\n*Early Life and Accession to the County*\n\nRaymond III's early life was shaped by the turbulent political landscape of the Levant. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli a prominent Crusader leader. Upon his father's death in 1154 Raymond inherited the County at the age of eleven. This presented a significant challenge as he had to navigate complex political intrigues and military conflicts with limited experience. His early years were characterized by struggles to consolidate his authority and quell rebellions within the County. He relied heavily on experienced military leaders and advisors to maintain stability.\n\nRaymond III’s reign witnessed significant military campaigns against Muslim forces. He engaged in numerous battles seeking to expand and defend Tripoli's territory. He formed alliances with other Crusader states and occasionally with Muslim rulers when it served his interests. His military successes helped to solidify his position as a formidable leader in the region. He also focused on strengthening Tripoli's defenses building fortifications and maintaining a well-equipped army.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nA key aspect of Raymond III's rule was his skillful diplomacy. He understood the importance of maintaining a balance of power in the region. He engaged in complex negotiations with various rulers including the kings of Jerusalem the sultans of Egypt and Syria and the emperors of the Byzantine Empire. He formed alliances with different powers at different times to achieve his political goals. His relationships with Saladin the renowned Muslim leader of Egypt and Syria were particularly notable. While often enemies in battle they engaged in periods of negotiation and even temporary truces.\n\nRaymond III’s diplomatic efforts extended beyond military alliances. He fostered trade and cultural exchange with neighboring regions promoting economic prosperity in Tripoli. He attracted merchants artisans and scholars to the County contributing to its growth and development. He also worked to maintain good relations with the local Muslim population ensuring stability and preventing unrest.\n\n*Later Years and Legacy*\n\nIn his later years Raymond III continued to face military challenges and political difficulties. He dealt with internal rebellions and external threats from various Muslim dynasties. He spent much of his time consolidating his power and securing Tripoli’s borders. He died in 1199 leaving the County to his nephew Raymond IV. Raymond III's legacy is that of a capable and resilient ruler who successfully defended Tripoli’s independence for many years. He is remembered as a skilled military commander a shrewd diplomat and a dedicated defender of the Crusader states. His reign represents a high point in the history of the County of Tripoli a period of relative stability and prosperity in a volatile region.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0011152960360050201}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.3841830625315197e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.24586127698421478}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.6201043128967285}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.08493684977293015}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.